1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
<font color="#20e020">HI removed, cleaned
and corrected by Lunar Team</font>

2
00:00:24,983 --> 00:00:27,443
He's from the AG's office.
We're catching a hot one.

3
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:29,862
Hooray. I was about to go mad
with boredom.

4
00:00:30,155 --> 00:00:31,905
Don't say, "Hooray". Someone's died.

5
00:00:32,449 --> 00:00:35,576
Well, if they have, my happiness
makes no difference to them.

6
00:00:35,744 --> 00:00:38,120
Nor does catching their killer,
for that matter.

7
00:00:38,288 --> 00:00:40,622
True, but it makes a difference
to the killer.

8
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:43,292
This e-mail came
to the attorney general's office...

9
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:45,544
...and the governor's office
20 minutes ago.

10
00:00:45,712 --> 00:00:49,131
"Pay attention.
At exactly 11:05 a.m. today...

11
00:00:49,299 --> 00:00:54,595
...at 35.04 north and 116.49 west...

12
00:00:54,763 --> 00:00:58,557
...you'll see an arrogant and greedy person
punished with death...

13
00:00:58,725 --> 00:01:02,227
...and you will know I am serious."
Signed, "Joe Q Public."

14
00:01:02,395 --> 00:01:04,146
- Interesting.
- It's probably nothing.

15
00:01:04,314 --> 00:01:08,275
- Why can't local police handle it?
- Some fool let the governor read the thing.

16
00:01:08,443 --> 00:01:10,819
He talks to the AG,
the AG talks to Minelli...

17
00:01:10,987 --> 00:01:13,655
...and Minelli tells me
we have to handle it personally.

18
00:01:13,823 --> 00:01:15,491
Rigsby, you're the fastest driver.

19
00:01:15,658 --> 00:01:17,826
If we leave now
we can get there by 11:05.

20
00:01:17,994 --> 00:01:20,954
- Where is it?
- Mojave Desert. Middle of nowhere.

21
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:23,540
- Quarter of a mile off the highway.
- Interesting.

22
00:01:23,708 --> 00:01:27,503
- Boring. Three hours away.
- Desert's beautiful this time of year.

23
00:01:29,923 --> 00:01:31,507
Let's all go.

24
00:01:32,967 --> 00:01:34,718
We can bring a picnic lunch.

25
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:42,726
Someone has to stay
and answer the phones.

26
00:01:59,994 --> 00:02:03,247
- How much further?
- About 50 feet.

27
00:02:04,207 --> 00:02:06,834
Look at that. Look what I found.

28
00:02:07,001 --> 00:02:08,585
What is it?

29
00:02:08,753 --> 00:02:12,172
It's a worm. It's a fossilized worm.

30
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:13,590
I'm happy for you.

31
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:16,885
All right, this is it. This is the spot.

32
00:02:18,221 --> 00:02:21,974
11:04 and change. Right on time.

33
00:02:22,392 --> 00:02:24,768
- For what?
- There's still fifteen seconds to go.

34
00:02:24,936 --> 00:02:29,523
You know, when this worm was alive,
this desert was the floor of a great sea.

35
00:02:29,691 --> 00:02:31,692
There were dinosaurs swimming
above us.

36
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:34,570
- That is kind of awesome.
- Yeah.

37
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:37,948
Sharks the size of buses.
Brilliantly colored sea monsters.

38
00:02:38,116 --> 00:02:41,827
Those mountains,
they would have been volcanoes.

39
00:02:41,995 --> 00:02:43,996
This is a goat turd,
about six months old.

40
00:02:46,207 --> 00:02:50,794
Still, giant sharks, here.

41
00:02:51,796 --> 00:02:55,424
Okay, we've done our job. Somebody
yanked our chain pretty good. Let's go.

42
00:02:56,092 --> 00:03:00,220
- Wait. I think we should wait.
- What for? What could possibly happen?

43
00:03:00,847 --> 00:03:04,474
I have no idea. But it's a pleasant spot,
and I have sandwiches.

44
00:03:04,642 --> 00:03:06,768
Eat them in the car.
We've wasted enough time.

45
00:03:08,188 --> 00:03:10,564
- Hey, do you hear that?
- Yeah.

46
00:03:10,732 --> 00:03:11,857
What is that?

47
00:03:30,376 --> 00:03:32,294
Our victim's name is David Whittaker.

48
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:36,048
One of a party of six skydivers that
went up with an instructor this morning.

49
00:03:36,216 --> 00:03:39,051
All executives of a company
called Carnelian Prime Trust...

50
00:03:39,219 --> 00:03:44,348
...out on an outdoor adventure retreat.
Our victim was VP of Human Resources.

51
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:50,020
David Whittaker, Carnelian Prime Trust.
Got it. I'll start digging.

52
00:03:51,105 --> 00:03:52,231
See that? Right here?

53
00:03:52,398 --> 00:03:56,443
That's the master strap, okay?
And it was cut nearly clean through.

54
00:03:56,611 --> 00:03:58,862
That has to be deliberate,
and that is evil.

55
00:03:59,030 --> 00:04:02,908
The minute this thing deployed,
the chute would have come clean off.

56
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:06,286
- Who rigged the chute?
- Me. I did. Last night.

57
00:04:06,454 --> 00:04:10,290
And I double-checked every one of them.
They were A-okay. I guarantee it.

58
00:04:10,875 --> 00:04:13,835
- This was done after.
- By someone that knows parachutes.

59
00:04:14,003 --> 00:04:15,337
Yes.

60
00:04:15,505 --> 00:04:19,258
I've been rigging chutes for 15 years.
I've never had one accident. Not one.

61
00:04:19,425 --> 00:04:21,176
There's a number on there.

62
00:04:21,344 --> 00:04:24,471
Is there any way to identify
which chute is for which jumper?

63
00:04:24,973 --> 00:04:26,598
Are they assigned to individuals?

64
00:04:26,766 --> 00:04:29,518
I tell the jumpers
to grab the first chute they come to.

65
00:04:29,686 --> 00:04:32,354
Those numbers are strictly
for the maintenance logs.

66
00:04:32,522 --> 00:04:35,023
Who had access to the chutes
after you rigged them?

67
00:04:35,191 --> 00:04:37,734
They're in the hangar overnight.
It's not locked.

68
00:04:37,902 --> 00:04:40,612
So anyone who had access
to the airport, basically.

69
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:42,864
Agent Lisbon, is it?

70
00:04:43,032 --> 00:04:47,035
We're leaving. Keep my office informed.
And you'll be hearing from our lawyers.

71
00:04:47,203 --> 00:04:50,664
- Do it now.
- Gonna let him get away with that?

72
00:04:50,832 --> 00:04:53,458
- Mr. Faulk.
- Hold on.

73
00:04:53,626 --> 00:04:55,460
This is a murder investigation.

74
00:04:55,628 --> 00:04:58,505
You can't leave
until we say you can leave.

75
00:05:00,174 --> 00:05:03,010
I apologize. I thought we had answered
all your questions.

76
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:08,682
Do we have your permission to leave,
Agent Lisbon?

77
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:11,268
Yes. But we're gonna have
follow-up questions.

78
00:05:11,436 --> 00:05:13,478
You know our location.

79
00:05:14,022 --> 00:05:16,773
Yes, we do.
We'll be in touch, Mr. Faulk.

80
00:05:26,409 --> 00:05:30,037
- Why isn't the body on the move yet?
- Forensics is still working the scene.

81
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:32,998
He fell from the sky.
What could they work on for an hour?

82
00:05:33,166 --> 00:05:35,250
Nerds, they won't be hurried.

83
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:39,421
So who is this Randall Faulk guy?

84
00:05:39,589 --> 00:05:43,175
President and CEO
of Carnelian Prime Trust.

85
00:05:43,343 --> 00:05:45,844
One of the top 10
private equity firms in the world.

86
00:05:46,012 --> 00:05:48,055
They buy and sell big companies.

87
00:05:48,222 --> 00:05:52,517
Oil, steel, auto plants, hotel chains.
You name it, they own it.

88
00:05:52,685 --> 00:05:53,769
Rich bastards.

89
00:05:53,936 --> 00:05:56,229
As soon as the scene's clear,
go to the airport.

90
00:05:56,397 --> 00:05:59,024
Find out who had access
to the parachutes last night.

91
00:05:59,484 --> 00:06:02,361
And find out about Spruell's history.
See if anything bumps.

92
00:06:02,528 --> 00:06:04,363
You got it, boss.

93
00:06:06,783 --> 00:06:07,949
Van Pelt, what's up?

94
00:06:08,117 --> 00:06:11,620
- You close to a TV?
- No. Why?

95
00:06:12,580 --> 00:06:14,456
Aw, hell.

96
00:06:15,291 --> 00:06:17,376
Want your sandwich?

97
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:29,096
Help you?

98
00:06:29,263 --> 00:06:31,765
- We're looking for Mr. Faulk.
- Right up at the house.

99
00:06:31,933 --> 00:06:34,184
- Thank you.
- Thanks.

100
00:06:35,395 --> 00:06:38,063
Following Mr. Whittaker's horrific death...

101
00:06:38,231 --> 00:06:40,941
...a message was sent
within the past hour to the police...

102
00:06:41,109 --> 00:06:43,944
...and several media outlets,
including Channel 4 News.

103
00:06:44,112 --> 00:06:46,738
It reads, "I have proved my strength.

104
00:06:46,906 --> 00:06:50,367
I have scored one for the little guy,
and I will do it again...

105
00:06:50,535 --> 00:06:53,912
...unless Carnelian Prime Trust
makes a public apology...

106
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:58,500
...for its greed and arrogance,
and promises to change its ways.

107
00:06:58,668 --> 00:07:00,669
Yours truly, Joe Q Public."

108
00:07:01,254 --> 00:07:03,880
- Illiterate nonsense.
- Perhaps.

109
00:07:04,048 --> 00:07:06,216
But the threat to your lives is explicit.

110
00:07:06,384 --> 00:07:08,927
We need to set up a security plan
with the marshal.

111
00:07:09,262 --> 00:07:12,013
What time are you planning
on going back to the city?

112
00:07:12,181 --> 00:07:13,765
We're not.

113
00:07:14,225 --> 00:07:16,560
We're staying here.
We're continuing the retreat.

114
00:07:16,727 --> 00:07:17,853
For God's sake, Rand.

115
00:07:18,271 --> 00:07:20,856
Perhaps there are other
considerations here?

116
00:07:21,023 --> 00:07:23,692
I'm not sure staying here
is the most advisable posture.

117
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:28,738
We stay. I'm not letting
some random lunatic disturb Carnelian.

118
00:07:29,198 --> 00:07:32,409
This is a test of our strength.
David would want us to continue.

119
00:07:32,577 --> 00:07:36,121
Yes, he would. Yes, he would.
David would never back down.

120
00:07:36,289 --> 00:07:40,542
David wouldn't want you to respectfully
acknowledge his tragic and untimely death?

121
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:42,502
Oh, well, okay.

122
00:07:43,087 --> 00:07:46,423
He wouldn't want it to interfere
with your kayaking schedule.

123
00:07:49,135 --> 00:07:52,637
- Joan, is it?
- Jane. Like the girl.

124
00:07:52,805 --> 00:07:55,474
- Jane.
- Mr. Jane.

125
00:07:56,559 --> 00:07:58,685
- Mr. Jane.
- Thank you.

126
00:07:59,228 --> 00:08:03,231
This week that we spend here
is not a vacation. This is a test.

127
00:08:03,399 --> 00:08:06,193
What we learn here about ourselves
and about each other...

128
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:09,321
...is at the core of the philosophy
of Carnelian's success.

129
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:13,783
There will come an appropriate moment
to mourn our dear friend.

130
00:08:15,244 --> 00:08:18,163
This is the moment
to show resolve and courage.

131
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:24,252
And now perhaps you can give us
a briefing on the case so far.

132
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:27,130
We investigate murders.
We don't give briefings.

133
00:08:27,548 --> 00:08:29,758
Why on earth not?
We have the right to know.

134
00:08:29,926 --> 00:08:32,719
- We're the victims here.
- David Whittaker is the victim.

135
00:08:32,887 --> 00:08:36,473
Any of us could have picked that chute.
Any of us could be dead right now.

136
00:08:36,641 --> 00:08:39,226
Yes. The saboteur didn't know
which of you would die.

137
00:08:39,393 --> 00:08:43,271
He felt that any one of you
would fit the bill.

138
00:08:43,439 --> 00:08:44,981
Greedy and arrogant.

139
00:08:46,192 --> 00:08:49,486
- How does that make you feel?
- Oh, is this group therapy?

140
00:08:49,654 --> 00:08:50,779
You want group therapy?

141
00:08:50,947 --> 00:08:53,365
No, we want a professional
police detective.

142
00:08:54,659 --> 00:08:57,619
I will be speaking with your superiors
to assure myself...

143
00:08:57,787 --> 00:09:00,997
- ...that your people are the best option.
- A good assurance to get.

144
00:09:01,165 --> 00:09:04,626
What is the worst thing
you people have done lately?

145
00:09:06,963 --> 00:09:08,880
We control assets of over $50 billion.

146
00:09:09,382 --> 00:09:12,175
Our companies employ
over half a million people.

147
00:09:12,343 --> 00:09:14,469
The way the economy's been going...

148
00:09:14,637 --> 00:09:17,514
...I've been forced to put 50,000
men and women out of work.

149
00:09:17,682 --> 00:09:21,059
Some of them will blame Carnelian
for their misfortunes, I'm sure.

150
00:09:21,227 --> 00:09:24,062
- It's understandable.
- Does that bother you, personally?

151
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:27,899
It most certainly does. I am concerned
about security just like anyone else.

152
00:09:28,067 --> 00:09:31,069
- I don't consider myself bulletproof.
- That's not what I meant.

153
00:09:31,237 --> 00:09:34,155
Does it bother you that people
are out of work on your say-so?

154
00:09:34,323 --> 00:09:36,741
No. That's my job.

155
00:09:37,368 --> 00:09:41,371
To make the tough decisions.
Tough, yet rational and ethical.

156
00:09:41,539 --> 00:09:45,041
And, yes, I'm afraid
people will suffer because of it.

157
00:09:45,334 --> 00:09:48,336
So it's no surprise that someone's
making a stand against you?

158
00:09:48,504 --> 00:09:52,799
No. There's always malcontents
amongst us. I'll send you our register.

159
00:09:52,967 --> 00:09:56,970
- You have a register of malcontents?
- Anyone who's sued us or threatened to.

160
00:09:57,138 --> 00:10:00,473
Written hostile letters, that sort of thing.
They're in the register.

161
00:10:00,641 --> 00:10:04,352
- It'd be good for us to take a look.
- Yeah.

162
00:10:07,106 --> 00:10:09,482
We'd like a list of everyone
with a security pass.

163
00:10:09,650 --> 00:10:12,235
- You have a warrant?
- It's not privileged information.

164
00:10:12,403 --> 00:10:15,864
- You can give it to us without prejudice.
- Well, okay, if you're sure.

165
00:10:16,032 --> 00:10:19,826
Yeah, I'm sure. if you could send it
to that address, please.

166
00:10:21,954 --> 00:10:24,581
Hey, you'll be getting the airport list
any second now.

167
00:10:24,749 --> 00:10:26,207
Okay.

168
00:10:27,918 --> 00:10:31,046
Got it. I'll crosscheck it
against the Carnelian list.

169
00:10:37,887 --> 00:10:40,847
One name on both lists. Lee Skelling.

170
00:10:41,349 --> 00:10:42,515
Lee Skelling?

171
00:10:42,683 --> 00:10:45,310
Yeah, he works the baggage truck
a couple days a week.

172
00:10:45,478 --> 00:10:47,771
- He's not here today.
- Okay, thank you.

173
00:10:47,938 --> 00:10:50,690
- Thanks, Van Pelt.
- Yep.

174
00:10:52,109 --> 00:10:56,404
Sniveling lackey pigs is what you are,
you know that? Damn lackeys.

175
00:10:56,572 --> 00:10:57,864
- Calm down, Lee.
- Make me.

176
00:10:59,450 --> 00:11:01,660
Stop right there.
You let him go right now, you hear me?

177
00:11:01,827 --> 00:11:03,620
It's not loaded.

178
00:11:04,038 --> 00:11:07,248
Now, you put that down right now,
you idiot.

179
00:11:07,667 --> 00:11:09,834
- But, Daddy...
- Get inside, boy.

180
00:11:16,759 --> 00:11:20,387
- Scared the pants off you, didn't he?
- Yeah, he did.

181
00:11:21,055 --> 00:11:22,931
No, he didn't.

182
00:11:28,062 --> 00:11:31,064
Two years ago, you sent this letter
to Carnelian headquarters.

183
00:11:31,232 --> 00:11:34,359
"You greedy sons of bitches
ought to know better than to steal...

184
00:11:34,527 --> 00:11:38,279
...from decent citizens like myself.
You're no better than scum in my book.

185
00:11:38,447 --> 00:11:40,949
You better pay up
or there's gonna be consequences."

186
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:43,118
- Recall writing this letter?
- Yes, I do.

187
00:11:43,577 --> 00:11:44,619
Tell me about it.

188
00:11:44,787 --> 00:11:49,791
Like the letter says,
which I wrote in a moment of anger...

189
00:11:49,959 --> 00:11:51,793
...they stole from me.

190
00:11:51,961 --> 00:11:55,964
From my family. When I called them on it,
they not only didn't pay me...

191
00:11:56,173 --> 00:11:58,550
...they did their damnedest
to throw me in jail.

192
00:11:58,718 --> 00:12:02,220
- How did they steal from you?
- I used to work at Grant Aerospace.

193
00:12:02,388 --> 00:12:07,267
Fan ducts for airliners.
They had a whole incentive thing going.

194
00:12:07,435 --> 00:12:11,146
If a floor guy comes up with an idea
that saves costs or speeds the line...

195
00:12:11,313 --> 00:12:14,232
...he gets a bonus.
Fifty thousand dollars.

196
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:16,901
I came up with an improvement
on the riveting gear...

197
00:12:17,069 --> 00:12:20,780
...that saved them five and a half cents
per unit. That's huge.

198
00:12:20,948 --> 00:12:23,992
- So 50,000 for me, right?
- That's a lot of money.

199
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:28,997
The CEO shook my hand.
I had it in writing.

200
00:12:29,165 --> 00:12:31,916
Then Carnelian Prime buys
Grant Aerospace.

201
00:12:32,084 --> 00:12:34,627
Come time to pay my bonus,
they welsh.

202
00:12:34,795 --> 00:12:37,464
I complained about it, they sacked me.

203
00:12:37,631 --> 00:12:41,009
- What did you do then?
- Oh, I tried to take them to court.

204
00:12:41,844 --> 00:12:44,512
Me and one old cross-eyed lawyer
out of the phone book...

205
00:12:44,722 --> 00:12:48,141
...against 20 Ivy League weasels.
No contest.

206
00:12:48,309 --> 00:12:51,644
- I didn't even get a hearing.
- Sounds like a bad injustice was done.

207
00:12:51,812 --> 00:12:56,983
- Sounds like you have a right to be angry.
- Yes, I do. I do have a right.

208
00:13:08,537 --> 00:13:11,164
Your friends already took Lee.
What do you want?

209
00:13:11,332 --> 00:13:13,208
Jessie Skelling?

210
00:13:13,375 --> 00:13:18,463
No, I'm Cindy Crawford.
Jessie's on vacation in Mexico.

211
00:13:32,228 --> 00:13:33,269
Ma'am?

212
00:13:34,063 --> 00:13:37,982
I can't even look at you people,
you make me so mad.

213
00:13:38,150 --> 00:13:40,360
Lee's done nothing. Nothing.

214
00:13:40,528 --> 00:13:43,655
Jessie, if your husband is innocent,
we're your best friends.

215
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:45,240
We'll prove he's innocent.

216
00:13:49,245 --> 00:13:50,286
Will you help us?

217
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:55,542
You want some water or a soda?

218
00:13:56,001 --> 00:13:58,086
Some water sounds lovely.

219
00:13:58,254 --> 00:14:01,506
Where were you the night before last,
from 9 p.m. till 9 the next morning?

220
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:04,050
I was at work and then I was at home.

221
00:14:04,218 --> 00:14:06,511
- At work at the airport?
- Yes.

222
00:14:06,679 --> 00:14:09,430
And, yes, I could have
gotten into Spruell's hangar...

223
00:14:09,598 --> 00:14:12,767
...and messed with that parachute,
but I didn't.

224
00:14:12,935 --> 00:14:16,437
I worked my shift, I went home,
and that's all I did.

225
00:14:17,273 --> 00:14:20,608
I didn't know those Carnelian people
were out there jumping that day.

226
00:14:20,776 --> 00:14:23,403
You know what?
I wouldn't give a damn if I did know.

227
00:14:23,571 --> 00:14:25,113
No?

228
00:14:26,448 --> 00:14:30,743
Okay, yeah, I would give a damn.
They could have given me that money.

229
00:14:30,911 --> 00:14:34,038
It was peanuts to them. Nothing.

230
00:14:34,206 --> 00:14:38,084
But cut a man's chute? No.

231
00:14:38,711 --> 00:14:40,044
Four years with the Rangers.

232
00:14:40,212 --> 00:14:43,715
- You're familiar with parachutes.
- Yes, I am.

233
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:47,093
- You even been in the military, Cho?
- Yes.

234
00:14:47,261 --> 00:14:48,970
Then you know.

235
00:14:49,138 --> 00:14:51,723
I would chop a man's head off
and pee down his neck...

236
00:14:51,891 --> 00:14:54,434
...before I'd ever cut his chute that way.

237
00:14:55,102 --> 00:14:59,564
I mean, that's bad.

238
00:15:00,649 --> 00:15:02,859
That's out there.

239
00:15:04,278 --> 00:15:09,490
Lee is a God-fearing man.
He wouldn't kill somebody like that.

240
00:15:09,658 --> 00:15:12,160
- No way.
- He was a soldier. He can kill.

241
00:15:12,328 --> 00:15:17,415
- For his country. Not for himself.
- What's the difference, you think?

242
00:15:17,958 --> 00:15:20,335
I don't know.
But it makes all the difference.

243
00:15:20,502 --> 00:15:23,588
You're very confident of his innocence.

244
00:15:23,756 --> 00:15:25,340
How can you be so sure?

245
00:15:27,509 --> 00:15:30,511
Because I asked him if he did it,
and he said, no, he didn't do it.

246
00:15:30,679 --> 00:15:34,515
Ah. You suspected
he might indeed have done it.

247
00:15:34,683 --> 00:15:37,894
- But he denied it. You believe him.
- Lee don't lie to me.

248
00:15:38,270 --> 00:15:41,814
He'll cause me trouble and grief
a hundred ways, but he don't lie to me.

249
00:15:43,275 --> 00:15:46,319
Wives often say that
about their husbands.

250
00:15:46,487 --> 00:15:49,364
Easy to fool yourself
that the people you love are honest.

251
00:15:50,950 --> 00:15:52,784
Yeah, it is.

252
00:15:53,827 --> 00:15:55,620
But I know he wouldn't lie.

253
00:15:55,788 --> 00:15:57,664
- Why?
- Because.

254
00:15:59,416 --> 00:16:02,543
- Why?
- Because I'm dying.

255
00:16:05,172 --> 00:16:09,884
He wouldn't lie to somebody who's gonna
sit next to the Almighty pretty soon.

256
00:16:11,553 --> 00:16:12,845
I'm sorry.

257
00:16:13,973 --> 00:16:16,474
Stuff happens. Don't worry about it.

258
00:16:19,561 --> 00:16:21,479
What time did Lee get home?

259
00:16:21,647 --> 00:16:25,441
Same time as every night,
about half past midnight.

260
00:16:32,074 --> 00:16:33,574
Yeah.

261
00:16:38,914 --> 00:16:41,249
My husband did not do this thing.

262
00:16:41,417 --> 00:16:44,752
If he went into jail,
what would happen to the kids?

263
00:16:45,963 --> 00:16:48,214
They'd go into care.

264
00:16:49,925 --> 00:16:51,551
And he wouldn't do that to me.

265
00:16:54,221 --> 00:16:56,055
He wouldn't.

266
00:16:57,474 --> 00:16:59,100
Thank you for your time.

267
00:17:02,730 --> 00:17:05,064
Your wife said
you wouldn't do that to her.

268
00:17:05,232 --> 00:17:09,861
I don't know. Maybe if you thought
you could get away with it, you would.

269
00:17:11,113 --> 00:17:15,116
When I was working at Grant Aerospace,
Jessie was getting the best treatment.

270
00:17:15,617 --> 00:17:19,245
If I hadn't made a nuisance of myself,
I'd still have my job...

271
00:17:19,413 --> 00:17:23,374
...and my health insurance.
Jessie would be okay.

272
00:17:23,959 --> 00:17:27,628
So why did I bitch and moan, right?

273
00:17:27,796 --> 00:17:31,966
I mean, why couldn't I just be quiet?
Keep my job?

274
00:17:35,095 --> 00:17:36,429
I don't know.

275
00:17:37,097 --> 00:17:41,726
Ego and pride and vanity, I expect.

276
00:17:43,479 --> 00:17:46,314
Yeah, I guess something like that.

277
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:52,528
But if I thought
I could get away with it?

278
00:17:53,572 --> 00:17:58,159
Yes, I would kill
every last one of those bastards.

279
00:17:58,994 --> 00:18:01,662
But you can't kill rich people
and get away with it.

280
00:18:04,249 --> 00:18:06,834
Everybody knows that.

281
00:18:07,044 --> 00:18:09,212
- I know. I can read your mind.
- Oh, you can?

282
00:18:09,379 --> 00:18:13,091
You're thinking Mr. Skelling
is innocent and we should release him. Cho.

283
00:18:13,258 --> 00:18:15,927
That's amazing.
That's exactly what I was thinking.

284
00:18:16,095 --> 00:18:19,430
Let me try. You're thinking:

285
00:18:19,598 --> 00:18:22,517
"Jane is right, the man is innocent.
We should let him go."

286
00:18:22,684 --> 00:18:26,687
No. I think you want him to be innocent
because his guilt would be too simple.

287
00:18:26,855 --> 00:18:30,108
- He has motive, opportunity and no alibi.
- Yeah...

288
00:18:30,275 --> 00:18:32,443
- Yeah, boss?
- Let Lee Skelling go.

289
00:18:32,611 --> 00:18:33,694
You sure?

290
00:18:34,196 --> 00:18:38,366
- We have no hard evidence against him.
- Okay, will do.

291
00:18:38,575 --> 00:18:40,618
We should talk
to the Carnelian executives.

292
00:18:40,786 --> 00:18:43,121
- Throw a cat among the pigeons.
- You think? Why?

293
00:18:43,288 --> 00:18:45,164
What if this is not what it looks like?

294
00:18:45,332 --> 00:18:47,625
- What if it's about something else?
- Like?

295
00:18:47,793 --> 00:18:50,128
- I don't know.
- So you're suggesting we throw...

296
00:18:50,295 --> 00:18:52,547
- ...a blind cat among the pigeons?
- Yeah.

297
00:18:53,465 --> 00:18:54,841
No.

298
00:19:18,031 --> 00:19:19,073
Howdy.

299
00:19:19,241 --> 00:19:21,576
I understand that you released
a promising suspect.

300
00:19:21,743 --> 00:19:23,828
Eh. We didn't like him for it.

301
00:19:23,996 --> 00:19:27,165
We think... Well, that is, I think.
Agent Lisbon disagrees with me.

302
00:19:27,332 --> 00:19:30,751
I think the answer lies
with one of you here.

303
00:19:30,919 --> 00:19:33,588
- Well, that's absurd.
- Why?

304
00:19:33,755 --> 00:19:37,008
You think one of us
sabotaged David's parachute?

305
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:39,385
Why not?
The chutes were clearly numbered.

306
00:19:39,553 --> 00:19:42,930
The saboteur would simply have
to make sure not to choose the dud.

307
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:45,391
But how could they know
who would pick the dud?

308
00:19:45,559 --> 00:19:48,436
They'd be killing
a randomly selected colleague.

309
00:19:48,604 --> 00:19:51,606
- Why would anyone wanna do that?
- Yes. I don't know.

310
00:19:51,773 --> 00:19:54,609
We'll find out.
So tell us a little about yourselves.

311
00:19:54,776 --> 00:19:57,528
Let's use this sadness
to get some real truths, shall we?

312
00:19:57,696 --> 00:20:02,074
- Joyce, you first.
- Me first to do what exactly?

313
00:20:02,534 --> 00:20:05,119
I'll tell you what I think,
to get the ball rolling.

314
00:20:05,287 --> 00:20:07,371
I think you've made your way
in the world...

315
00:20:07,539 --> 00:20:12,126
...by concealing your true feelings
under a mask of positivity and niceness.

316
00:20:12,294 --> 00:20:15,171
But underneath, you're a seething mass
of ugly, bitter resentment.

317
00:20:17,883 --> 00:20:21,427
You think Faulk is a stupid buffoon,
but you're far too clever...

318
00:20:21,595 --> 00:20:24,639
- ...to be brave enough to tell him so.
- How dare you?

319
00:20:24,806 --> 00:20:26,807
And you, Mr. Braemar.

320
00:20:26,975 --> 00:20:29,310
- Marine Corps, yes?
- Yes.

321
00:20:29,478 --> 00:20:32,939
Office politics must seem
kind of trivial in comparison.

322
00:20:34,816 --> 00:20:37,151
You fantasize about
pulling a couple of weapons...

323
00:20:37,319 --> 00:20:39,654
...and showing these civilians
a little reality?

324
00:20:39,821 --> 00:20:42,156
That is baseless, inappropriate,
and I resent it.

325
00:20:42,324 --> 00:20:45,243
Agent Lisbon,
this is a high-profile case.

326
00:20:45,410 --> 00:20:48,829
A career-making case
if concluded successfully.

327
00:20:48,997 --> 00:20:52,083
Is this how you wish to proceed?
With clownish games?

328
00:20:52,251 --> 00:20:54,961
I apologize. Mr. Jane is a consultant.

329
00:20:55,128 --> 00:20:58,631
His statements in no way reflect
the official view of the CBI.

330
00:20:58,799 --> 00:21:02,176
I am simply trying to get a full picture
of the group dynamic here.

331
00:21:02,344 --> 00:21:07,014
Anyone resent the sexual relationship
between Faulk and Miss Sobell here?

332
00:21:08,016 --> 00:21:09,934
Anyone?

333
00:21:13,939 --> 00:21:15,815
Why would anyone resent it?

334
00:21:15,983 --> 00:21:17,858
Don't engage with him, Nadia.

335
00:21:18,026 --> 00:21:20,736
- Oh, you let him tell you what to do?
- That's enough.

336
00:21:23,532 --> 00:21:26,033
- You can go now.
- Yes, we can.

337
00:21:31,456 --> 00:21:32,999
You threw a cat, all right.

338
00:21:33,166 --> 00:21:36,377
What valuable insight did you glean
as a result?

339
00:21:36,545 --> 00:21:40,047
Nothing. It's a little disappointing.

340
00:21:40,215 --> 00:21:44,715
Well, maybe, just maybe, this case
is exactly what it looks like it's about.

341
00:21:44,803 --> 00:21:47,179
Bitter unsubs with a grudge
against the fat cats.

342
00:21:47,347 --> 00:21:49,223
Nice drive, though.

343
00:21:49,641 --> 00:21:52,059
Nice three-hour drive.

344
00:21:52,728 --> 00:21:55,271
I'm hungry. You hungry?

345
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:06,282
- Ready?
- Ready.

346
00:22:08,327 --> 00:22:11,662
Know what's weird about those guys?
None of them seem to give a damn.

347
00:22:11,830 --> 00:22:15,166
A colleague of theirs falls out of the sky
and they seem okay with it.

348
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:16,751
Is that guilt or indifference?

349
00:22:16,918 --> 00:22:19,420
Corporate brainwashing.
Turns them into robots.

350
00:22:19,588 --> 00:22:23,591
- Grief isn't productive, and that's all.
- I don't buy that.

351
00:22:23,759 --> 00:22:26,427
People make up their own minds.
You can't brainwash them.

352
00:22:26,595 --> 00:22:29,555
Sure, you can. That's what these
corporate retreats are about.

353
00:22:29,723 --> 00:22:32,350
It's primitive brainwashing
via group suffering.

354
00:22:32,517 --> 00:22:36,354
It's like office karaoke
or fraternity hazing.

355
00:22:36,521 --> 00:22:37,563
How so?

356
00:22:37,731 --> 00:22:42,777
When the individual is humiliated,
their perceived value of the group is raised.

357
00:22:43,904 --> 00:22:47,948
I went on a retreat when I got promoted
to head of the unit. I wasn't humiliated.

358
00:22:48,116 --> 00:22:49,909
- I wasn't brainwashed.
- So you say.

359
00:22:50,077 --> 00:22:54,205
- I wasn't. it was useful, actually.
- How so?

360
00:22:54,373 --> 00:22:58,626
Learning about communication
and leadership skills. Building trust.

361
00:22:58,835 --> 00:23:01,253
Something you could profit from.

362
00:23:02,631 --> 00:23:03,798
What does that mean?

363
00:23:03,965 --> 00:23:07,134
Like you don't know
you have major trust issues?

364
00:23:07,302 --> 00:23:09,637
I trust people. I trust you.

365
00:23:10,263 --> 00:23:12,973
No, you don't. I don't trust you either.

366
00:23:18,146 --> 00:23:20,439
It's upsetting to hear that.

367
00:23:21,441 --> 00:23:23,109
- You don't trust me?
- Of course not.

368
00:23:23,276 --> 00:23:26,779
How many times have you lied to me?
Misled me? Tricked me?

369
00:23:26,947 --> 00:23:29,448
- Is that trust? No.
- Well, we have to remedy this.

370
00:23:29,616 --> 00:23:32,785
- Let's do a trust fall.
- A trust fall?

371
00:23:32,953 --> 00:23:35,246
Yeah, I'm sure you did one
on your CBI retreat.

372
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:37,498
It's when you fall backward
and I catch you.

373
00:23:37,666 --> 00:23:39,291
Oh, yes. We did do that.

374
00:23:39,459 --> 00:23:40,835
- No.
- You won't?

375
00:23:41,002 --> 00:23:43,921
- We have a long drive still.
- Here we have two coworkers...

376
00:23:44,089 --> 00:23:47,174
...recognizing the boundaries
of their professional relationship.

377
00:23:47,384 --> 00:23:50,177
You wanna trust me,
but there's something holding you back.

378
00:23:50,387 --> 00:23:53,931
Yes. You're untrustworthy.
It's my job not to trust you.

379
00:23:54,099 --> 00:23:56,976
Lisbon, I want you to know
that you can trust me.

380
00:23:57,144 --> 00:24:01,355
No matter what happens,
I will be there for you. I will.

381
00:24:02,023 --> 00:24:03,274
I need you to know that.

382
00:24:05,193 --> 00:24:07,278
- Now, can I catch you?
- Come on.

383
00:24:07,446 --> 00:24:08,863
Please?

384
00:24:09,448 --> 00:24:10,489
Fine.

385
00:24:14,369 --> 00:24:15,411
Come on.

386
00:24:18,039 --> 00:24:20,207
See? You can trust me.

387
00:24:20,375 --> 00:24:22,710
Oh, wow, that worked.
Suddenly I trust you.

388
00:24:22,878 --> 00:24:24,044
Aha.

389
00:24:24,212 --> 00:24:27,339
I allow you to drive me
around the country in this contraption.

390
00:24:27,507 --> 00:24:28,716
That's serious trust.

391
00:24:35,557 --> 00:24:38,559
That was
a classic rendition of "Paris Nights."

392
00:24:38,727 --> 00:24:42,897
- Now it's back to jazz on KRA...
- Talk to me.

393
00:24:43,064 --> 00:24:44,398
Do I have to?

394
00:24:44,566 --> 00:24:51,322
No, I can just fall asleep and we can drift
into oncoming traffic. Your call.

395
00:24:56,369 --> 00:25:00,831
- Have you seen any good movies lately?
- No. You?

396
00:25:02,167 --> 00:25:03,209
No.

397
00:25:05,378 --> 00:25:07,505
Interesting.

398
00:25:21,269 --> 00:25:24,897
Lisbon, we just got another message
from Joe Q Public.

399
00:25:25,065 --> 00:25:31,946
"When all go to bed
they best say their goodbyes."

400
00:25:32,113 --> 00:25:35,741
"For prompt at my bidding,
all things will rise."

401
00:25:36,701 --> 00:25:41,413
"And the king of the rats
will meet his demise."

402
00:25:41,581 --> 00:25:43,415
Signed, "Joe Q Public."

403
00:25:43,583 --> 00:25:45,543
- Is that all?
- Yeah, that's all.

404
00:25:45,710 --> 00:25:48,462
- Okay.
- Okay, bye.

405
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:51,966
Now he's writing bad poetry.

406
00:25:52,133 --> 00:25:54,802
What does that mean,
"All things will rise"?

407
00:25:56,429 --> 00:25:57,805
Oh.

408
00:26:08,316 --> 00:26:09,984
What? What does it mean?

409
00:26:10,151 --> 00:26:12,111
He's planted a bomb to kill Faulk.

410
00:26:44,102 --> 00:26:45,144
What the hell?

411
00:26:46,896 --> 00:26:48,564
- What are you doing?
- Bear with us.

412
00:26:48,732 --> 00:26:50,858
- We have reason to believe...
- There it is.

413
00:26:52,193 --> 00:26:54,028
- Told you so.
- There's what?

414
00:26:54,195 --> 00:26:57,323
There's no need to gloat.
There is an explosive device...

415
00:26:57,490 --> 00:26:58,991
- ...under your bed.
- Oh, my God.

416
00:26:59,159 --> 00:27:00,284
- Wait.
- Wait?

417
00:27:00,452 --> 00:27:01,952
Just let me get a better look.

418
00:27:03,455 --> 00:27:07,249
No, I don't think there's
a pressure trigger. You can get up now.

419
00:27:07,417 --> 00:27:08,876
- You don't think?
- It doesn't.

420
00:27:09,044 --> 00:27:11,920
Timer's set to go off in 10 minutes,
we have plenty of time.

421
00:27:12,088 --> 00:27:14,089
If you'll just get out of the bed slowly.

422
00:27:14,257 --> 00:27:17,551
We do not wanna jar the device
in any way.

423
00:27:17,719 --> 00:27:19,386
Boom.

424
00:27:20,472 --> 00:27:22,014
For God's sakes.

425
00:27:22,182 --> 00:27:25,267
Sorry, I couldn't resist.

426
00:27:40,241 --> 00:27:43,160
- It didn't go off. What happened?
- She's a nasty little girl.

427
00:27:43,328 --> 00:27:46,413
Anyone inside of 20 feet
would have been a red mist.

428
00:27:46,581 --> 00:27:48,082
See this wire right here?

429
00:27:48,249 --> 00:27:51,085
Yeah, it came loose at some point,
disarmed her.

430
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:53,587
- Silly mistake.
- Lucky, huh?

431
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:57,508
Don't touch that.
Even though it's disarmed...

432
00:27:57,676 --> 00:28:00,177
...if you touch the red terminal
we're all barbecue.

433
00:28:00,345 --> 00:28:03,222
Oh, well, that's good to know.

434
00:28:03,431 --> 00:28:05,432
- Looks like expert work.
- Absolutely.

435
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:09,853
You've got high-test Army ordnance
combined with a hobby-store trigger.

436
00:28:10,021 --> 00:28:12,064
Whoever built this
really knows his onions.

437
00:28:12,232 --> 00:28:13,816
- Army ordnance?
- Yeah.

438
00:28:13,983 --> 00:28:17,695
You've got the innards of five Mark I-9
Army-issue grenades packed together.

439
00:28:21,741 --> 00:28:25,536
Soon as he heard about the bomb,
he knew you people would be on him.

440
00:28:25,704 --> 00:28:27,871
So he grabbed his gear
and he lit out of here.

441
00:28:28,039 --> 00:28:31,750
- How did he know about the bomb?
- I told him.

442
00:28:31,918 --> 00:28:35,170
I listen to the police scanner.
Kind of a hobby.

443
00:28:35,338 --> 00:28:37,673
You hear some things, my God.

444
00:28:37,841 --> 00:28:41,009
- Where did he go? His truck's still here.
- Friend gave him a ride.

445
00:28:41,177 --> 00:28:43,470
- Friend's name?
- Sure, like I'm gonna tell you.

446
00:28:43,638 --> 00:28:45,931
Lee's only making things worse
by running.

447
00:28:46,099 --> 00:28:47,182
Right.

448
00:28:48,351 --> 00:28:52,521
He said to tell you that he is
100 percent innocent. He didn't do it.

449
00:28:52,689 --> 00:28:54,857
But that's no matter,
once the law gets you.

450
00:28:55,024 --> 00:28:57,609
- You know where's he gone?
- Wouldn't you like to know.

451
00:28:57,777 --> 00:29:00,529
- I know. Just wondering if you do.
- Yeah? Where'd he go?

452
00:29:00,697 --> 00:29:02,197
The mountains.

453
00:29:03,742 --> 00:29:05,784
You're welcome to look.
You won't find him.

454
00:29:05,952 --> 00:29:08,579
I bet you're right.
Capable man, your husband.

455
00:29:08,747 --> 00:29:10,706
- Yes, he is.
- Is he armed?

456
00:29:11,207 --> 00:29:13,917
If he is, it's for hunting and nothing else.

457
00:29:14,753 --> 00:29:17,379
Send him my best wishes
next time you speak to him.

458
00:29:18,131 --> 00:29:19,882
I will.

459
00:29:25,972 --> 00:29:28,891
- Damn it, I should have never let him go.
- He didn't do it.

460
00:29:29,058 --> 00:29:31,810
Maybe he didn't,
but it sure looks like he did.

461
00:29:31,978 --> 00:29:35,022
Rigsby, put out an APB on Lee Skelling.

462
00:29:35,190 --> 00:29:38,108
He's armed and dangerous,
headed for the mountain regions.

463
00:29:38,276 --> 00:29:40,152
Didn't do it.

464
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:43,655
Carnelian is a company
founded on good American values.

465
00:29:43,823 --> 00:29:46,033
We will be a better company
because of this.

466
00:29:46,201 --> 00:29:50,871
The sick individual behind this campaign
against us will not affect us in any way.

467
00:29:51,039 --> 00:29:53,332
We will not be intimidated.

468
00:29:53,875 --> 00:29:55,125
Fool.

469
00:29:55,293 --> 00:29:57,377
Playing the hero. He'll get himself killed.

470
00:29:57,545 --> 00:30:00,130
Carnelian needs to turn this
to their PR advantage...

471
00:30:00,298 --> 00:30:03,383
- ...or their business will lose money.
- How's Carnelian's stock?

472
00:30:03,551 --> 00:30:05,719
Down 15 percent
since Whittaker's death.

473
00:30:05,887 --> 00:30:08,388
Wow, I wonder whether anyone
made money off the drop.

474
00:30:08,556 --> 00:30:10,224
- Shorting the stock you mean?
- Yeah.

475
00:30:10,391 --> 00:30:13,477
If you'd known Carnelian
was gonna be targeted by Joe Public...

476
00:30:13,645 --> 00:30:15,187
...because you are Joe Public...

477
00:30:15,355 --> 00:30:17,856
...betting against them on the market
is easy money.

478
00:30:18,024 --> 00:30:19,817
Can't be SkelLing. He's got no money.

479
00:30:19,984 --> 00:30:21,902
It's not Skelling. We know that already.

480
00:30:22,070 --> 00:30:24,196
You know that.
We're keeping an open mind.

481
00:30:26,324 --> 00:30:28,283
On it. I'll run the market trading logs.

482
00:30:28,451 --> 00:30:30,494
See if anybody made a killing,
so to speak.

483
00:30:36,376 --> 00:30:37,626
Any word on Skelling?

484
00:30:37,794 --> 00:30:40,379
Couple potential sightings
in the Mount Whitney area.

485
00:30:40,547 --> 00:30:42,714
Parks services are all over it.
We'll get him.

486
00:30:42,882 --> 00:30:45,551
I doubt it.
Skelling's in his element up there.

487
00:30:45,718 --> 00:30:50,347
His survivalist fantasy life come true.
He's gone aground like a grizzly bear.

488
00:30:51,057 --> 00:30:55,060
Eureka. I found a trading account that
made a series of moves in the market...

489
00:30:55,228 --> 00:30:56,687
...against Carnelian stock.

490
00:30:56,855 --> 00:31:00,858
- The trades exactly mirror our time line.
- There a name attached to the account?

491
00:31:01,025 --> 00:31:04,111
There's a subsidiary track
to a British bank.

492
00:31:04,279 --> 00:31:06,822
A company account
in the name of NS Holdings.

493
00:31:06,990 --> 00:31:09,616
NS? Nadia Sobell.

494
00:31:09,784 --> 00:31:11,577
Aha.

495
00:31:12,036 --> 00:31:14,121
We're looking for Nadia Sobell.

496
00:31:14,289 --> 00:31:16,748
She's on the mountain with the others,
playing war.

497
00:31:16,916 --> 00:31:19,543
- War?
- Yeah, you know, those paintball guns?

498
00:31:19,711 --> 00:31:24,214
You divide into teams, get dressed up
and creep around trying to kill each other.

499
00:31:24,382 --> 00:31:28,385
It's actually a lot of fun.
If only I hadn't twisted my ankle.

500
00:31:28,553 --> 00:31:32,472
So, what do you guys want
to talk to Nadia for?

501
00:32:10,094 --> 00:32:11,136
Nadia?

502
00:32:12,847 --> 00:32:14,514
Hey.

503
00:32:15,850 --> 00:32:17,267
Nadia.

504
00:32:18,186 --> 00:32:20,062
Oh, my God.

505
00:32:44,879 --> 00:32:46,296
Okay, thanks, guys.

506
00:32:47,131 --> 00:32:49,883
- What do we have?
- Body went to the lab a couple hours ago.

507
00:32:50,051 --> 00:32:52,052
Local PD's working a search
at the scene.

508
00:32:52,512 --> 00:32:54,429
- Any word on Skelling?
- Nothing recent.

509
00:32:54,597 --> 00:32:58,350
Last sighting of him was mid-morning
at a trailhead three and a half miles...

510
00:32:58,518 --> 00:33:01,395
...from where Sobell was shot.
He could have made it, easy.

511
00:33:01,813 --> 00:33:05,857
- Coincidence. He didn't do it.
- We don't... Never mind.

512
00:33:06,025 --> 00:33:09,319
- Whoever did this thinks we're idiots.
- Yes. A mistake.

513
00:33:09,487 --> 00:33:12,864
How so? We look like idiots when
our number one suspect gets shot...

514
00:33:13,032 --> 00:33:15,826
- ...practically right in front of us.
- Not in front of us.

515
00:33:15,994 --> 00:33:18,161
- Any messages from Joe Public?
- Not a word.

516
00:33:18,329 --> 00:33:19,788
Won't hear from him.

517
00:33:19,956 --> 00:33:22,124
That disguise has grown boring,
I suspect.

518
00:33:22,291 --> 00:33:25,961
Joe Public's a ruse? What's the motive
for killing Whittaker and Sobell?

519
00:33:26,129 --> 00:33:29,131
Yes, well, that's the question.
Whittaker's death was random.

520
00:33:29,298 --> 00:33:31,341
Any of them could have taken
the dud chute.

521
00:33:31,509 --> 00:33:34,636
But Sobell's wasn't.
What does that tell us?

522
00:33:36,139 --> 00:33:40,100
- What? We give up.
- Well, I'm not entirely sure.

523
00:33:41,728 --> 00:33:44,938
- Are Faulk and the others still inside?
- Yeah.

524
00:33:48,860 --> 00:33:52,195
- What's in the bag?
- Oh. Picnic lunch.

525
00:33:52,363 --> 00:33:53,947
- What's in the bag?
- A bomb.

526
00:33:54,115 --> 00:33:55,157
Ha, ha.

527
00:33:55,324 --> 00:33:59,494
- What's in the bag, seriously?
- Seriously, a bomb.

528
00:33:59,912 --> 00:34:01,121
What happened?

529
00:34:01,289 --> 00:34:03,915
Sobell and I were competing
against De Shaun and Joyce.

530
00:34:04,083 --> 00:34:06,293
I was tracking Joyce
when I heard the shot.

531
00:34:06,461 --> 00:34:09,921
And then I heard De Shaun yelling
from over the ridge.

532
00:34:12,091 --> 00:34:15,469
Mr. Faulk? Your cars are here
and the baggage is loaded.

533
00:34:15,636 --> 00:34:17,262
- Thank you, Jake.
- You're leaving?

534
00:34:17,430 --> 00:34:20,390
Yes. It was a mistake to stay.

535
00:34:20,558 --> 00:34:24,394
I was tempting fate to prove a point,
and now poor Nadia has paid for it.

536
00:34:24,854 --> 00:34:27,272
- It's too much.
- You can't blame yourself.

537
00:34:27,440 --> 00:34:29,107
Why not?

538
00:34:29,275 --> 00:34:30,984
- Excuse me?
- No, it's all right.

539
00:34:32,570 --> 00:34:36,823
The barb is well placed. I've been wrong.
The company's been wrong.

540
00:34:36,991 --> 00:34:39,201
I need to issue a statement.
Make an apology.

541
00:34:39,368 --> 00:34:41,953
No. No, you can't let this psycho win.

542
00:34:42,121 --> 00:34:44,915
No, not to Joe Public,
to the general public. The people.

543
00:34:45,083 --> 00:34:48,001
Carnelian is gonna change its ways
and become a better company.

544
00:34:48,169 --> 00:34:50,712
Redeemed by its suffering.
Reborn, rebranded.

545
00:34:50,880 --> 00:34:53,256
Ethical, honest, clean.

546
00:34:53,424 --> 00:34:56,843
- That's brilliant.
- Yes, it is. It's brilliant.

547
00:34:57,011 --> 00:34:58,678
No, it's just necessary.

548
00:34:59,889 --> 00:35:02,682
I want a campaign on those themes
ready by next week.

549
00:35:02,850 --> 00:35:04,559
I got a phone call from Van Pelt.

550
00:35:04,727 --> 00:35:07,312
Forensics got a hit on the bullet
taken out of Sobell.

551
00:35:07,480 --> 00:35:12,818
It's a boat-tail .264 caliber bullet
manufactured by Winchester in 1989.

552
00:35:12,985 --> 00:35:15,654
Part of a custom batch
given to the top 10 finalists...

553
00:35:15,822 --> 00:35:19,199
...in the Annual American Cowboy
Shootist competition in Reno, Nevada.

554
00:35:19,367 --> 00:35:22,202
- You're kidding.
- Let me guess.

555
00:35:22,537 --> 00:35:24,496
- Jake Cooby.
- Seventh place.

556
00:35:24,664 --> 00:35:29,084
- Jake Cooby? The ranch manager?
- Of course. It all adds up.

557
00:35:29,252 --> 00:35:31,002
- It's perfect.
- It is?

558
00:35:31,170 --> 00:35:34,714
- Why would Cooby wanna kill somebody?
- Well, let's go ask him.

559
00:35:35,216 --> 00:35:39,136
Mr. Faulk, where does Mr. Cooby
put his feet up?

560
00:35:39,303 --> 00:35:41,263
- The staff kitchen.
- Thank you.

561
00:35:41,430 --> 00:35:43,014
Wait.

562
00:35:43,474 --> 00:35:44,641
Why is Jake involved?

563
00:35:45,476 --> 00:35:48,311
The bullet that killed Nadia,
apparently very special.

564
00:35:48,479 --> 00:35:50,647
Almost certainly belongs to Mr. Cooby.

565
00:35:50,815 --> 00:35:52,566
Jake? Can't be.

566
00:35:52,733 --> 00:35:55,360
- You know him well?
- I've been coming here for six years.

567
00:35:55,528 --> 00:35:57,654
We're good friends. This is a good man.

568
00:35:57,822 --> 00:35:59,781
Salt of the earth. This can't be.

569
00:36:00,575 --> 00:36:02,826
Forensics doesn't lie.

570
00:36:02,994 --> 00:36:04,870
He never expressed animosity
toward you?

571
00:36:05,037 --> 00:36:06,413
No. Never.

572
00:36:07,707 --> 00:36:10,083
Must be a secret grudge.

573
00:36:10,376 --> 00:36:13,003
Staff kitchen, you said. This way?

574
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:18,175
- Maybe you can help us.
- Of course.

575
00:36:18,342 --> 00:36:20,760
- Come with me when I talk to Mr. Cooby.
- All right.

576
00:36:20,928 --> 00:36:23,430
Hold on. I don't think
that's such a good idea.

577
00:36:23,931 --> 00:36:26,850
It's him that Cooby's angry with.
Let's use that. Vex him.

578
00:36:27,018 --> 00:36:29,186
- Open him up a little.
- It's too risky.

579
00:36:29,353 --> 00:36:33,690
You can wait outside with your gun drawn
in case Cooby gets a little haywire.

580
00:36:40,323 --> 00:36:42,699
You saying it's me? I shot her?

581
00:36:43,326 --> 00:36:46,411
Yes, and cut Whittaker's parachute,
and planted the bomb...

582
00:36:46,579 --> 00:36:49,456
- ...that almost killed Mr. Faulk here.
- I surely did not.

583
00:36:49,624 --> 00:36:53,501
- Jeez, why in heck would I do all that?
- That's what we'd like to know.

584
00:36:53,669 --> 00:36:58,089
Are you guys for real? Come on.
How long have you known me, Mr. Faulk?

585
00:36:58,257 --> 00:36:59,591
Why, Jake?

586
00:36:59,759 --> 00:37:03,470
What did I ever do to you to justify this?
Help me understand.

587
00:37:03,971 --> 00:37:07,015
Okay. Now, see here.

588
00:37:07,767 --> 00:37:10,268
I don't know how,
but you got the wrong idea about me.

589
00:37:10,436 --> 00:37:11,811
Is that right?

590
00:37:11,979 --> 00:37:15,190
Then why was it that one of your
prize bullets killed Nadia Sobell?

591
00:37:15,358 --> 00:37:19,027
- It was?
- And why did you build this clever thing?

592
00:37:19,195 --> 00:37:21,655
What? is that a bomb?

593
00:37:22,198 --> 00:37:25,659
- Yes, Jake, it's your bomb. You made it.
- No, I didn't.

594
00:37:25,826 --> 00:37:28,828
- What have you been smoking, man?
- Really? No? It's not?

595
00:37:28,996 --> 00:37:30,163
This is not your trigger?

596
00:37:31,499 --> 00:37:32,899
- Oh, for God's sakes.
- Is that...?

597
00:37:32,917 --> 00:37:36,711
It's okay. Everyone just stay calm.
It's no problem.

598
00:37:37,588 --> 00:37:41,299
I'm pretty sure you disarm it by removing
this wire from the red terminal...

599
00:37:41,467 --> 00:37:43,551
No. Idiot.

600
00:37:45,888 --> 00:37:47,931
There. What the hell are you playing at?

601
00:37:48,099 --> 00:37:49,599
Aha.

602
00:37:49,767 --> 00:37:52,519
- As they say.
- What are you talking about?

603
00:37:53,479 --> 00:37:55,188
You knew how to disarm the bomb.

604
00:37:55,815 --> 00:37:57,315
Because you made it.

605
00:37:57,900 --> 00:38:01,486
You planted it to divert suspicion
from yourself.

606
00:38:01,654 --> 00:38:05,115
Ergo, it was you that killed Whittaker
and Sobell.

607
00:38:06,826 --> 00:38:10,203
I hate to spoil your theory,
but it's obvious how to disarm the bomb.

608
00:38:10,371 --> 00:38:14,332
No, it wasn't. I bet 90 percent of people
would think to remove this wire here...

609
00:38:14,500 --> 00:38:15,625
No!

610
00:38:15,793 --> 00:38:18,712
The bomb squad took out the explosive.
That's just Play-Doh.

611
00:38:18,879 --> 00:38:21,339
And you are a wretched, scheming,
cold-hearted murderer.

612
00:38:22,883 --> 00:38:25,051
- Where do you keep your tea?
- In the cabinet.

613
00:38:25,636 --> 00:38:28,138
- You're mad.
- Thank you.

614
00:38:28,806 --> 00:38:32,142
Oh, and could you please ask
Agent Lisbon to come in?

615
00:38:32,310 --> 00:38:34,144
Thanks, Mr. Cooby.

616
00:38:37,940 --> 00:38:41,234
- You have no legal proof whatsoever.
- Eh.

617
00:38:41,694 --> 00:38:44,571
Legal proof will be found, no doubt.

618
00:38:44,739 --> 00:38:48,408
But personally, I don't need it.
I just like to know that I'm right.

619
00:38:50,328 --> 00:38:53,455
Sir, put your hands behind your back.

620
00:38:53,622 --> 00:38:56,374
You're under arrest. Let's go.

621
00:39:13,642 --> 00:39:16,603
I knew it was you as soon as the bomb
under your bed failed.

622
00:39:16,771 --> 00:39:20,690
- It was too clever, Randy. Too clever.
- Don't call me Randy.

623
00:39:20,858 --> 00:39:24,194
But why would you randomly
wanna kill one of your own executives?

624
00:39:24,362 --> 00:39:25,862
Doesn't make sense.

625
00:39:26,030 --> 00:39:31,826
Then Sobell was shot
and I saw your whole game, Randy.

626
00:39:32,703 --> 00:39:34,037
That's very childish.

627
00:39:34,205 --> 00:39:38,750
Whittaker's death was merely camouflage
to disguise Sobell's death.

628
00:39:38,918 --> 00:39:40,418
She's who you needed dead.

629
00:39:41,504 --> 00:39:43,671
I won't say anything
until my lawyer arrives.

630
00:39:44,215 --> 00:39:47,342
Suffice to say, you have no proof.

631
00:39:47,510 --> 00:39:50,512
The question is,
what did Sobell have on you?

632
00:39:50,679 --> 00:39:53,932
- What was she going to do?
- Nothing.

633
00:39:54,892 --> 00:39:58,853
That was a rhetorical question.
We know everything we need to now.

634
00:39:59,021 --> 00:40:01,731
This is an affidavit
from Nadia Sobell's lawyer.

635
00:40:03,150 --> 00:40:04,984
Nadia was secretly negotiating
to join KBT...

636
00:40:05,152 --> 00:40:08,112
...your number one rival. She was also
drafting a sexual-harassment suit...

637
00:40:08,239 --> 00:40:10,073
...against you to coincide
with her departure.

638
00:40:10,241 --> 00:40:13,076
That's why she was shorting
Carnelian stock. You needed to kill her.

639
00:40:13,244 --> 00:40:17,622
Because nobody gets on top
of Randall Faulk, do they?

640
00:40:17,790 --> 00:40:19,582
Nobody.

641
00:40:21,127 --> 00:40:22,502
Where's my lawyer?

642
00:40:23,129 --> 00:40:27,424
But I gotta tell you,
the whole Joe Q Public ruse, genius.

643
00:40:29,176 --> 00:40:32,470
I couldn't see how you could profit
from attacking your own company.

644
00:40:32,638 --> 00:40:37,100
But you, you have vision.
Carnelian reborn and rebranded.

645
00:40:37,852 --> 00:40:42,063
Everybody loves a comeback.
Everybody loves redemption.

646
00:40:43,774 --> 00:40:46,192
- You have nothing.
- Enough to rip you to pieces.

647
00:40:46,360 --> 00:40:48,862
And you know it.
So before your lawyer gets here...

648
00:40:49,029 --> 00:40:53,283
...and ties everybody's hands,
let's make a deal.

649
00:40:59,915 --> 00:41:02,292
- I'm listening.
- Good.

650
00:41:02,460 --> 00:41:05,253
We'll call Whittaker manslaughter one.

651
00:41:05,421 --> 00:41:08,047
We'll forget about the bomb...

652
00:41:08,632 --> 00:41:11,176
...and we won't go after
the death penalty.

653
00:41:15,931 --> 00:41:18,558
In exchange for what?

654
00:41:26,150 --> 00:41:27,442
Bills.

655
00:41:27,610 --> 00:41:29,819
What's this?

